<article class="env-remark" id="3730BF">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/3730BF">Remark <span data-tag="3730BF">7.1.6.7</span>.</a> Our terminology does not quite conform to the usage in classical topology. Recall that if $X$ is a compact metric space, the <em>shape</em> of $X$ is defined as a pro-object in the <em>homotopy</em> category of spaces. There is a refinement of shape, known as <em>strong shape</em>, which takes values in the homotopy category of pro-spaces. Definition <a href="/tag/086B90" data-tag="086B90">7.1.6.3</a> is a generalization of strong shape, rather than shape. We refer the reader to <span class="cite">[<a href="/bibliography/shapetheory">shapetheory</a>]</span> for a discussion of classical shape theory. </p>
</article>